chunks and\n   work through them one at a time, verifying each on disk as you go.\n4. Your turn is NOT complete until every required artifact exists and has passed\n   your own verification. Do not end your turn with unverified or pending work.\n\n[logs/translation.log]\nReading additional input from stdin...\n{\"type\":\"thread.started\",\"thread_id\":\"01a03548-2d72-77a1-99f4-9f251277b945\"}\n{\"type\":\"turn.started\"}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_0\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"I’m using the Brazil build guidance because the workspace instructions prohibit direct Cargo commands. I’ll first inventory the C sources and build metadata, then translate and independently verify every created file before compiling with the permitted build path.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /local/home/scheschb/.codex/plugins/cache/aim/AmazonBuilderCoreAIAgents-core/1.0.6504269516/skills/brazil/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /local/home/scheschb/.codex/plugins/cache/aim/AmazonBuilderCoreAIAgents-core/1.0.6504269516/skills/brazil/SKILL.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: brazil\\ndescription: Amazon's internal build system for Java/Kotlin (brazil-gradle), TypeScript/JS (npm-pretty-much), Python (brazil-python), Rust (CargoBrazil), Go, C/C++ (brazil-cmake). Use when working with Brazil packages, workspaces, dependencies, version sets, build commands, or encountering build errors and dependency conflicts.\\nversion: 1.0.0\\ntags: [skill, brazil, build-system, dependencies, java, kotlin, typescript, javascript, python, go, rust, c-cpp, cargobrazil, cargo-brazil]\\n---\\n\\n# Brazil Build System\\n\\n## Overview\\nBrazil is Amazon's internal build system providing a unified interface for diverse build technologies (npm-pretty-much, brazil-gradle, brazil-python, etc.). It manages dependencies, coordinates package versions through version sets, and supports reproducible builds across languages.\\n\\n**Main Documentation:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/\\n\\n## Usage\\nWhen working with Amazon internal development and encountering:\\n- Package creation, configuration, or dependency management\\n- Workspace setup or build errors\\n- Version conflicts or dependency resolution issues\\n- Language-specific build system configuration\\n- Publishing packages or setting up CI/CD\\n- Commands like `brazil-build`, `brazil workspace`, `brazil versionset`\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\n### 1. Identify the Language First\\nCheck the package's `Config` file for `build-system` to determine which language guide to reference:\\n\\n- `brazil-gradle`, `happytrails`, `happiertrails` → **[Java/Kotlin](references/language/java-kotlin.md)**\\n- `npm-pretty-much` → **[TypeScript/JavaScript](references/language/typescript-javascript.md)**\\n- `brazil-python` → **[Python](references/language/python.md)**\\n- `brazil-cmake` → **[C/C++](references/language/c-cpp.md)**\\n- `bgo-wrap-make` → **[Go](references/language/go.md)**\\n- `cargo-brazil-build` → **[Rust](references/language/rust.md)**\\n\\nYou **MUST** reference the appropriate language guide for language-specific commands, dependencies, and troubleshooting.\\n\\n### 2. Reference Workflow Guides as Needed\\n- **[Workspace Setup](references/workspace-setup.md)** - Creating workspaces, using packages, workspace structure\\n- **[Package Management](references/package-management.md)** - Creating packages, configuring dependencies, publishing\\n- **[Dependency Management](references/dependency-management.md)** - Resolving conflicts, debugging dependencies, Gordian Knot\\n- **[Build and Test](references/build-and-test.md)** - Building packages, running tests, build output\\n- **[Version Sets](references/version-sets.md)** - Creating or managing version sets\\n- **[CLI Commands](references/cli-commands.md)** - Comprehensive Brazil CLI command reference\\n\\n## Core Concepts\\n\\n### Packages\\nThe basic unit of code in Brazil containing source code, build configuration, and dependency information.\\n\\n**Key characteristics:**\\n- Each package is a separate git repository\\n- Lives in workspace `src/` directory\\n- Owned by teams via bindles, not individuals\\n\\n**For details on package structure and Config files:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-package/ and https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-config-file/\\n\\n### Workspaces\\nContainer for one or more packages you're working on, providing isolation and controlled build environment.\\n\\n**Structure:**\\n```\\nWorkspaceName/\\n├── src/\\n│   ├── PackageA/  (git repo)\\n│   └── PackageB/  (git repo)\\n└── [workspace metadata]\\n```\\n\\n**Critical rule:** For single-package builds, you **MUST** navigate into the specific package directory (`cd src/PackageName`) before running build commands. For building all packages, use `brazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build` from any package in the Brazil workspace instead.\\n\\n### Version Sets\\nCollections of package versions that are known to work together, enabling consistent builds across environments.\\n\\n**Purpose:**\\n- Define package versions that work together\\n- Coordinate dependency updates across teams\\n- Enable consistent builds\\n\\n**For details:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-version-set/\\n\\n### Dependencies\\nBrazil manages package dependencies to ensure required code is available during build and runtime.\\n\\n**Dependency Models:**\\n- **Classic Brazil**: Dependencies declared in Config file - https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/\\n- **Peru**: Language-native dependency management (e.g., package.json for npm, requirements.txt for Python) - https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/peru-user-guide/\\n\\n**Types:**\\n- **Regular dependencies**: Required for compilation/runtime, exported to consumers\\n- **Test dependencies**: Only for testing, not exported\\n- **Runtime dependencies**: Required at runtime but not compile time\\n- **Conflict resolution**: Use `resolves-conflict-dependencies` to specify versions when conflicts occur\\n\\n**For details:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/concepts-dependencies/\\n\\n### Build Systems\\nBrazil supports multiple language-specific build systems. The most common are:\\n- **Java/Kotlin**: brazil-gradle, happytrails/happiertrails\\n- **TypeScript/JavaScript**: npm-pretty-much\\n- **Python**: brazil-python\\n- **Ruby**: brazil-rake\\n- **Go**: bgo-wrap-make\\n- **Rust**: brazil-cargo\\n- **C/C++**: brazil-cmake\\n- **Scala**: brazil-sbt, happiertrails\\n\\nSome packages use wrapper build systems (e.g., `cdk-build`) or fully custom ones (e.g., `custom-build`). If the build system is not recognized, check the package README or ask the user for guidance on how to build.\\n\\n## Common Workflows\\n\\n### Building an Existing Package in a workspace\\n**When user already has a workspace with the package:**\\n1. Confirm you are in a brazil workspace with `brazil workspace show`\\n2. Navigate to package: `cd src/PackageName`\\n3. Build: `brazil-build release`\\n\\n**To build all packages at once with the release target** (note: the same can be done with other targets like `test`):\\n```bash\\nbrazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build release\\n```\\n\\n### Creating a New Workspace from Scratch and building\\n**Only when creating a new workspace:**\\n1. Find a package's primary version set at https://code.amazon.com/packages/PackageName/releases using ReadInternalWebsite\\n2. Create workspace: `brazil workspace create --name MyWorkspace --versionSet MyVersionSetGroup/MyVersionSetName`. You **MUST** specify a version set, ask the user if you cannot find a primary version set.\\n3. Use package: `brazil workspace use --package PackageName`\\n4. Navigate to package: `cd src/PackageName`\\n5. Build: `brazil-build release`\\n\\n### Resolving Dependency Conflicts\\n1. Identify conflict via build error\\n2. Use Gordian Knot: `gk-fix-mv-conflicts`\\n3. Or manually add to Config:\\n   ```\\n   resolves-conflict-dependencies = {\\n       1.0 = {\\n           ConflictPackage = 2.x;\\n       };\\n   };\\n   ```\\n4. Rebuild: `brazil-build release`\\n\\n### Adding Dependencies\\n1. Find package: https://code.amazon.com/packages/search\\n2. Find package major version number at https://code.amazon.com/packages/PackageName/releases using ReadInternalWebsite\\n3. Add to Config file dependencies section\\n4. If version set errors occur: first try `brazil ws sync -md`, then `brazil workspace merge` if still needed\\n5. Build: `brazil-build release`\\n\\n## CLI Validation Rules\\n\\nBefore executing any `brazil*` command, check:\\n\\n1. **Command exists**: Known `brazil` top-level subcommands include: `workspace`/`ws`, `versionset`/`vs`, `package`/`pkg`, `packagebuilder`/`pb`, `prefs`, `s3binary`/`s3`, `sandbox-build`, `setup`, `version`. Do NOT invent subcommands like `brazil merge`. If unsure whether a subcommand exists, run `brazil --help` to verify.\\n2. **Correct nesting**: It's `brazil workspace merge`, NOT `brazil merge`. It's `brazil versionset print`, NOT `brazil print`.\\n3. **Flag validity**: When unsure if a flag exists, run `<command> --help` first. Do NOT invent flags.\\n4. **Working directory**: `brazil-build`, `brazil-path`, and `brazil-context package` subcommands MUST run from inside a package directory. `brazil ws` commands work from anywhere in the workspace.\\n5. **Build system awareness**: `brazil-build` has NO flags of its own — it passes all arguments to the build system. The first argument is often called the \\\"target\\\" (e.g., in `brazil-build release`, `release` is the target). Do NOT use flag syntax like `brazil-build --target release`.\\n6. **Interactive commands**: `brazil setup` (all variants) and `brazil ws delete` require interactive input and will hang or prompt in automated environments. Do NOT run these commands directly — instruct the user to run them manually in their terminal. See the \\\"Interactive Commands\\\" section for the full list.\\n\\n## Interactive Commands (Do NOT Run Directly)\\n\\nThese commands require interactive input and MUST NOT be run by the agent:\\n\\n- `brazil setup` (all variants: `--java`, `--node`, `--python`, `--all`, `--core`)\\n- `brazil setup platform-support` — interactive by default. Instruct the user to run it in their terminal with `--force --mode overlay` (macOS) or `--force --mode legacy` (Windows) to skip prompts.\\n- `brazil ws delete` — prompts for confirmation (warn user about data loss)\\n\\nThe agent MAY run `brazil ws remove --force -p <pkg>` only after the user has explicitly confirmed they want the package removed.\\n\\nWhen the user needs to run any interactive command, instruct them to run it manually in their terminal.\\n\\n## Output Handling Rules\\n\\n| Tool | Handling |\\n|---|---|\\n| `brazil-build`, `brazil-recursive-cmd` | Redirect to log file, tail last 50 lines |\\n| `brazil-runtime-exec`, `brazil-farm` | Redirect to log file, tail last 50 lines |\\n| `brazil-package-cache` | Read stdout directly (small JSON); check stderr on failure |\\n| `brazil ws`, `brazil vs`, `brazil pkg`, `brazil pb`, `brazil-context`, `brazil-path` | Read output directly |\\n\\n## Decision Tree: Problem → Command\\n\\n| Problem | Command |\\n|---|---|\\n| Build single package | `brazil-build release` (from package dir) |\\n| Build all packages | `brazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build release` |\\n| Find workspace root | `brazil-context workspace root` |\\n| Find package name | `brazil-context package name` |\\n| Check dependency model (brazil vs peru) | `brazil-context workspace model` |\\n| Find current platform | `brazil-context workspace platform` |\\n| View build dependency graph | `brazil-path build.show-graph` (from package dir) |\\n| View runtime dependency graph | `brazil-path run.show-graph` (from package dir) |\\n| Java classpath | `brazil-path build.classpath` (from package dir) |\\n| Python path | `brazil-path build.pythonpath.python<version>` (from package dir, e.g. `python3.11`) |\\n| Add missing dependency | Edit Config, then `brazil ws sync -md`, then `brazil ws merge` if still needed |\\n| Resolve version conflicts | `gk-fix-mv-conflicts` (requires Gordian Knot — if command not found, tell user to install via `toolbox install gordian-knot`) |\\n| Is package in version set? | `brazil vs print --versionSet <VS>` and grep, or `brazil pkg listversionsets --package <Pkg> --majorVersion <MV>` |\\n| Check build request status | `brazil pb view --buildRequest <id>` |\\n| Submit dry-run build | `brazil ws dryrun` |\\n| Sync workspace to latest | `brazil ws sync` |\\n| Run cmd in runtime env (classic) | `brazil-runtime-exec <cmd>` |\\n| Run cmd in runtime env (Peru) | `brazil-farm -p <pkg> -c <closure>` |\\n\\n## brazil-context\\n\\nPrefer `brazil-context` over `findup` and `get-current-platform` — it runs faster by avoiding unnecessary loading of version set files. However, some queries need to load the version set, and that is where `brazil-path` is useful.\\n\\nNote: `brazil-context package` subcommands must be run from inside a package directory.\\n\\nSee **[CLI Commands](references/cli-commands.md)** for the full `brazil-context` command listing.\\n\\n## brazil-path Quick Reference\\n\\nMUST run from inside a package directory. Syntax: `brazil-path <graph>.<recipe>`\\n\\nSee **[CLI Commands](references/cli-commands.md)** for common recipes. For anything beyond those, run `brazil-path --help` or `brazil-path -list` from inside a package directory, or see https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/docs/brazil/user-guide/tools-brazil-path/\\n\\n## Quick Reference\\n\\n| Task | Command |\\n|------|---------|\\n| Create workspace | `brazil workspace create --name NAME --versionSet VS/name` |\\n| Use package | `brazil workspace use --package PackageName` |\\n| Show workspace | `brazil workspace show` |\\n| Build package | `brazil-build release` |\\n| Run tests | `brazil-build test` |\\n| Clean build | `brazil-build clean` |\\n| Fix conflicts | `gk-fix-mv-conflicts` |\\n| Sync metadata | `brazil ws sync -md` |\\n| Merge dependencies | `brazil workspace merge` |\\n| Print version set | `brazil versionset print --versionSet NAME` |\\n\\n## Common Mistakes\\n\\n### Building from Workspace Root\\n**Problem:** Running `brazil-build` from workspace root instead of package directory.\\n**Fix:** For a single package, always `cd src/PackageName` before building. To build all packages, run `brazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build` from anywhere in the brazil workspace.\\n\\n### Missing Dependencies After Config Changes\\n**Problem:** Added dependencies to Config but build fails with \\\"not in version set\\\" errors.\\n**Fix:** First try `brazil ws sync -md` to refresh metadata and to sync the metadata to the TIP of the version set. If that doesn't resolve it, run `brazil workspace merge` to submit a merge build. Make it clear to the user that they will need to merge the package into the version set themselves long-term.\\n\\n### Incorrect or unsure which Major Version to use\\n**Problem:** Added dependencies to Config but unsure which major version to use.\\n**Fix:** Use ReadInternalWebsite on https://code.amazon.com/packages/PackageName/releases to find available Major Versions\\n\\n### Ignoring Build Output Volume\\n**Problem:** Verbose build output overwhelms context, degrading conversation quality.\\n**Fix:** Redirect to log file: `brazil-build release > build.log 2>&1 ; tail -n 50 build.log`\\n\\n### Resolves Not Working\\n**Problem:** `resolves-conflict-dependencies` entries are ignored.\\n**Fix:** Use `gk-fix-mv-conflicts --cleanup` to rebuild conflict resolutions, or ensure resolved version exists in dependency graph.\\n\\n### Wrong Build System Commands\\n**Problem:** Using language-specific commands without checking build system.\\n**Fix:** Check Config file `build-system` field, then reference appropriate language guide.\\n\\n### Gordian Knot Not Installed\\n**Problem:** `gk-fix-mv-conflicts` command not found.\\n**Fix:** Install Gordian Knot via toolbox: `toolbox install gordian-knot`\\n\\n### Confusing ws merge and vs merge\\n**Problem:** Using `brazil vs merge` when intending to update local workspace, or vice versa.\\n**Fix:** `brazil ws merge` updates your local workspace's version set (by submitting a merge build). `brazil vs merge` modifies the shared remote version set — use with caution.\\n\\n## Support Resources\\n- **Documentation**: https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/brazil/user-guide/\\n- **CTI**: Developer Tools | Brazil\\n- **Email**: brazil-users@amazon.com\\n- **Package Search**: https://code.amazon.com/packages/search\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /local/home/scheschb/.codex/plugins/cache/aim/AmazonBuilderCoreAIAgents-core/1.0.6504269516/skills/brazil/references/language/rust.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /local/home/scheschb/.codex/plugins/cache/aim/AmazonBuilderCoreAIAgents-core/1.0.6504269516/skills/brazil/references/language/rust.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Rust with CargoBrazil\\n\\nCargoBrazil integrates Rust with Amazon's Brazil build system, managing toolchains and dependencies while preserving standard Cargo workflows.\\n\\n## Quick Start\\n\\n### Create a New Rust Package\\n\\nUse CreateHub to generate from template:\\n```bash\\n# Via toolbox\\ntoolbox install create-pkg\\ncreate-pkg --template DuplicatorRustPackage --name MyPackage\\n\\n# Or via CreateHub web UI\\n# https://createhub.corp.amazon.com/templates/DuplicatorRustPackage\\n```\\n\\nThis creates a properly configured package with Config, Cargo.toml, and .gitignore already set up.\\n\\n### Essential .gitignore\\n\\n```gitignore\\n# Generated by CargoBrazil - never commit\\nCargo.lock\\n/build\\ntarget/\\n/rust-toolchain.toml\\n\\n# IDE artifacts\\n.idea/\\n*.iml\\n```\\n\\n### First Build\\n\\n```bash\\nbrazil ws create --root --vs YourVersionSet\\ncd src/YourPackage\\nbrazil-build sync      # Setup (run after clone or metadata changes)\\nbrazil-build release   # Full build: tests + lints + docs\\n```\\n\\nAfter first build, standard `cargo` commands work directly:\\n```bash\\ncargo build            # Fast iteration\\ncargo test             # Run tests\\ncargo clippy           # Linting\\ncargo fmt              # Format code\\n```\\n\\n## Workspace Structure\\n\\n### Single Crate Package\\n\\n```\\nMyPackage/\\n├── Config             # Brazil configuration\\n├── Cargo.toml         # Rust manifest\\n├── src/\\n│   └── lib.rs         # or main.rs for binary\\n└── build-tools/bin/   # Optional: build scripts\\n```\\n\\n### Multi-Crate Workspace\\n\\n```\\nMyPackage/\\n├── Config\\n├── Cargo.toml         # Workspace manifest\\n├── crates/\\n│   ├── core/\\n│   │   ├── Cargo.toml\\n│   │   └── src/\\n│   └── cli/\\n│       ├── Cargo.toml\\n│       └── src/\\n└── build-tools/bin/\\n```\\n\\nWorkspace Cargo.toml:\\n```toml\\n[workspace]\\nresolver = \\\"2\\\"\\nmembers = [\\\"crates/*\\\"]\\n\\n[workspace.package]\\nversion = \\\"0.1.0\\\"\\nedition = \\\"2021\\\"\\n\\n[workspace.dependencies]\\n# Shared dependencies with versions\\nserde = { version = \\\"1.0\\\", features = [\\\"derive\\\"] }\\ntokio = { version = \\\"1.0\\\", features = [\\\"full\\\"] }\\n```\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n### Third-Party (crates.io)\\n\\nAdd directly to `Cargo.toml` - no Config or BTPT needed:\\n```toml\\n[dependencies]\\nserde = { version = \\\"1.0\\\", features = [\\\"derive\\\"] }\\ntokio = { version = \\\"1.0\\\", features = [\\\"full\\\"] }\\nanyhow = \\\"1.0\\\"\\n\\n[dev-dependencies]\\nmockall = \\\"0.11\\\"\\nproptest = \\\"1.0\\\"\\n```\\n\\nDependencies come from internal mirror (ThirdPartyCratesIoIndex), which typically lags 1-3 days behind crates.io.\\n\\n### First-Party (Amazon packages)\\n\\n1. Add to Config:\\n```\\ndependencies = {\\n    1.0 = {\\n        SomeRustPackage = 1.x;\\n    };\\n};\\n```\\n\\n2. Reference in Cargo.toml with `amzn-` prefix:\\n```toml\\n[dependencies]\\namzn-some-crate = \\\"*\\\"  # Version from Config/version set\\n```\\n\\n**Important:** The `amzn-` prefix is mandatory. CargoBrazil maps this to the actual crate name from the version set.\\n\\n### Native Libraries\\n\\nFor C/C++ dependencies (OpenSSL, zlib, etc.):\\n\\n```\\ndependencies = {\\n    1.0 = {\\n        Openssl = default;\\n        Zlib = 1.x;\\n    };\\n};\\n```\\n\\nCargoBrazil automatically:\\n- Sets `PKG_CONFIG_PATH` for discovery\\n- Configures linker search paths\\n- Sets library-specific env vars (`OPENSSL_LIB_DIR`, `OPENSSL_INCLUDE_DIR`)\\n\\n**Twisted builds error:**\\n```\\nDenying call to cc due to possible silent vendoring in crate '<crate>'\\n```\\n\\n**Fix:** The crate is compiling C code instead of using Brazil's library. Add the corresponding native library to your Config dependencies:\\n- `openssl-sys` → add `Openssl = default`\\n- `curl-sys` → add `Curl = 7.x`\\n- `bzip2-sys` → add `Bzip2 = 1.0`\\n\\n**Not your direct dependency?** Try `brazil ws sync --md` - an upstream library may have fixed it.\\n\\nFull guide: https://w.amazon.com/bin/view/Rust/Campaigns/TwistedBuilds/\\n\\n## Publishing a Library\\n\\nTo make your crate consumable by other packages:\\n\\n### Step 1: Add Target\\n\\n```\\ntargets = {\\n    MyPackage-1.0 = { type = rust; };\\n};\\n```\\n\\n### Step 2: Configure Cargo.toml\\n\\n```toml\\n[package]\\nname = \\\"amzn-mypackage\\\"  # Must use amzn- prefix\\nversion = \\\"0.1.0\\\"\\nedition = \\\"2021\\\"\\npublish = [\\\"brazil\\\"]\\nexclude = ['/build', '/Config', '/build-tools/']\\n\\n[lib]\\nname = \\\"amzn_mypackage\\\"  # Underscores for Rust identifier\\n```\\n\\n### Step 3: Verify\\n\\nAfter building, consumers can add to their Cargo.toml:\\n```toml\\n[dependencies]\\namzn-mypackage = \\\"*\\\"\\n```\\n\\n## Common Tasks\\n\\n### Adding Build Tools\\n\\nCommon tools to add to your Config `build-tools` section:\\n\\n**For faster builds:**\\n- `Mold = 2.x` - Faster linking (Linux, ~2-5x speedup)\\n- `Cargo-nextest = 0.x` - Faster parallel test execution\\n\\n**For code quality:**\\n- `Grcov = 0.8.x` - Test coverage reporting\\n\\n**Example config:**\\n```\\nbuild-tools = {\\n    0.1 = {\\n        CargoBrazil = 1.0;\\n        RustLang1x = 1.x;\\n        Grcov = 0.8.x;\\n        Cargo-nextest = 0.x;\\n        Mold = 2.x;\\n    };\\n};\\n```\\n\\n**Security scanning and unused dependency detection** require additional setup beyond build-tools. See https://w.amazon.com/bin/view/Rust/AuditingDependencies for CargoEevee and cargo-machete configuration.\\n\\n### Test Coverage\\n\\n**Setup:**\\n1. Add `Grcov = 0.8.x` to build-tools\\n2. Run coverage:\\n\\n```bash\\ncargo brazil with-coverage test --workspace  # Run instrumented tests\\ncargo brazil with-coverage report            # Generate HTML report\\n# Report at: build/private/cargo-target/coverage/\\n```\\n\\n**Require minimum coverage:**\\n```toml\\n# Cargo.toml\\n[package.metadata.cargobrazil]\\nmin-line-coverage = 80\\n```\\n\\nBuild fails if coverage drops below threshold.\\n\\n### Compiler Flags\\n\\nUse `.cargo/config.toml` (NOT the RUSTFLAGS env var, which CargoBrazil manages):\\n\\n```toml\\n[build]\\nrustflags = [\\\"-C\\\", \\\"target-feature=+crt-static\\\"]\\n\\n# For specific targets\\n[target.x86_64-unknown-linux-musl]\\nrustflags = [\\\"-C\\\", \\\"target-feature=+crt-static\\\"]\\n```\\n\\n**For environment variables in build scripts:** See https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-extend/#setting-environment-variables for injecting custom env vars via configure override.\\n\\n### Using Build Scripts\\n\\nBuild scripts (`build.rs`) work normally. For scripts needing Brazil context:\\n\\n```rust\\n// build.rs\\nfn main() {\\n    // These are set by CargoBrazil during configure\\n    println!(\\\"cargo:rerun-if-env-changed=BRAZIL_BUILD_HOME\\\");\\n    \\n    // Access Brazil dependencies via standard methods\\n    let openssl_dir = std::env::var(\\\"OPENSSL_LIB_DIR\\\")\\n        .expect(\\\"OPENSSL_LIB_DIR not set\\\");\\n}\\n```\\n\\n**Warning:** Do not call `brazil-path` from build scripts. Use environment variables instead.\\n\\n## Testing\\n\\n### Faster Tests with Nextest\\n\\nAfter adding `Cargo-nextest = 0.x` to build-tools:\\n\\n```bash\\ncargo nextest run          # Parallel test execution\\ncargo nextest run -j 8     # Control parallelism\\n```\\n\\n### Doc Tests\\n\\n```bash\\ncargo test --doc           # Run doc examples\\nbrazil-build build         # Generates docs during build phase\\n```\\n\\n## Troubleshooting\\n\\n### Crate/Version Not Found\\n\\n```bash\\ncargo brazil versions-for-crate --package <crate-name>\\n```\\n\\n- **Version not listed** - Mirror lags 1-3 days. Use available version or wait.\\n- **Crate not found** - Not synced. Run `brazil ws sync --md && cargo brazil fetch --target all`\\n- **1P crate not found** - Missing Config dependency. Add to Config dependencies.\\n- **`amzn-*` not resolved** - Missing prefix. Ensure Cargo.toml uses `amzn-` prefix.\\n\\n### Runtime Errors\\n\\n**\\\"envroot\\\" or \\\"interpreter\\\" errors:**\\n\\nCargoBrazil wraps binaries for Apollo deployment. For other environments:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Local execution: use unwrapped binary\\n./build/bin/<binary>.orig\\n\\n# Or use Brazil runtime wrapper\\nbrazil-runtime-exec <binary>\\n```\\n\\n**For Docker/ECS/Lambda:** Two options:\\n\\n1. **Use `--no-ld-wrapper`** (simplest, but breaks dynamic linking):\\n   Create `build-tools/bin/cargo-brazil-on-install`:\\n   ```bash\\n   #!/usr/bin/env bash\\n   set -euo pipefail\\n   cargo brazil install --bins --locked --no-ld-wrapper --path .\\n   ```\\n\\n2. **Use ApolloShimSetup** (preserves dynamic linking):\\n   Add runtime dependency and configure shim. See https://w.amazon.com/bin/view/ContainerBuild/ApolloShimSetup\\n\\n### Build Fleet Failures\\n\\n**\\\"Works locally, fails on build fleet\\\":**\\n\\n- **Rust version mismatch** - Use Cloud Desktop or match RustLang1x version\\n- **Missing build-tools** - Add locally-installed tools to Config\\n- **Flaky tests** - Use nextest with retries: `--retries 2`\\n\\n**Full clean and rebuild (wash cycle):**\\n```bash\\nbrazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages --reverse brazil-build clean\\nbrazil ws clean\\nbrazil-recursive-cmd --allPackages brazil-build release\\n```\\n\\n### Dependency Conflicts\\n\\n**Version mismatch between workspace crates:**\\n```bash\\ncargo tree -d  # Show duplicates\\ncargo tree -i <crate>  # Show inverse dependencies\\n```\\n\\n**Lock file issues:**\\n```bash\\nrm Cargo.lock\\nbrazil-build sync\\n```\\n\\n### Debug Logging\\n\\n**CargoBrazil build diagnostics:**\\n```bash\\nbrazil-build -v release    # Debug level\\nbrazil-build -vv release   # Trace level\\n\\n# Or via environment variable\\nCARGO_BRAZIL_LOG=amzn_cargo_brazil=debug,cargo_brazil=debug brazil-build\\n```\\n\\n**Your Rust application logs:**\\nMost Rust crates use `tracing` or `log` with `RUST_LOG` env var:\\n```bash\\nRUST_LOG=debug ./build/bin/my-binary\\nRUST_LOG=my_crate=debug,other_crate=warn ./build/bin/my-binary\\n```\\n\\n## IDE Setup\\n\\n### VS Code\\n\\nInstall extensions:\\n- rust-analyzer (required)\\n- CodeLLDB (debugging)\\n- Even Better TOML (syntax)\\n\\nAfter `brazil-build sync`, rust-analyzer works automatically.\\n\\n**Cloud Desktop (CDD/CDM):** rust-analyzer requires additional setup:\\n```bash\\naxe init builder-tools\\n```\\nOr configure rust-analyzer server path manually. See https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/ides-vscode/#remote-ssh-on-a-clouddesk-cdm\\n\\n### RustRover / IntelliJ\\n\\n1. Open package directory as project\\n2. Run `brazil-build sync` first\\n3. Trust the project when prompted\\n\\n**Common issue:** If rust-analyzer can't find deps, run `brazil-build sync`.\\n\\n## Platform Notes\\n\\n- **Linux (AL2/AL2023)** - Brazil-managed (RustLang1x). Matches build fleet, recommended.\\n- **macOS (Intel)** - Local rustup. Install matching version manually.\\n- **macOS (ARM)** - Local rustup. Some deps may need Rosetta.\\n- **Cloud Desktop** - Brazil-managed. Best for consistent results.\\n\\n**macOS setup:**\\n```bash\\nrustup default stable\\nrustup component add clippy rustfmt\\n```\\n\\n## Key Commands\\n\\n```bash\\n# Build phases\\nbrazil-build sync      # Setup/refresh environment\\nbrazil-build build     # Docs + artifacts only (fast)\\nbrazil-build release   # Full: tests + lints + docs + artifacts\\nbrazil-build test      # Tests only\\nbrazil-build lint      # Clippy + fmt check\\n\\n# Cargo Brazil utilities\\ncargo brazil fetch --target all          # Fetch deps for all platforms\\ncargo brazil versions-for-crate -p NAME  # Check crate availability\\ncargo brazil print target-dir            # Get cargo target directory\\ncargo brazil install --bins              # Install binaries to build/bin/\\n\\n# Coverage (requires Grcov)\\ncargo brazil with-coverage test --workspace\\ncargo brazil with-coverage report\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Advanced Topics\\n\\n### CargoBrazil.toml Configuration\\n\\nHelps tuning CargoBrazil behavior: parallelism, coverage scope, lint targets. Place this file in package root.\\n\\n```toml\\n[build]\\n# Parallel compiler frontend (experimental, can cause issues)\\nparallel-rustc = false\\n\\n[build.brazil-fleet]\\n# Disable incremental compilation on fleet (recommended - saves memory)\\ndisable-incremental = true\\n\\n[test]\\n# Coverage instrumentation scope\\n# \\\"workspace\\\" (recommended): Faster, only instruments your crates\\n# \\\"all\\\": Complete coverage including dependencies\\ninstrumentation-scope = \\\"workspace\\\"\\n\\n[lint]\\n# What to lint: \\\"default\\\" (libs/bins) or \\\"all\\\" (includes tests/benches)\\ncargo-targets = \\\"default\\\"\\n\\n# Features to enable during linting\\nfeatures = [\\\"feature1\\\", \\\"feature2\\\"]\\n```\\n\\n### Cross-Compilation\\n\\nFor building against different architectures (musl, Android, iOS):\\n\\n**Setup:**\\n1. Add stdlib: `RustStdMusls = 1.0` (for musl targets)\\n2. Add cross-linker: `CrossGCC = gunpowder`\\n3. Configure `.cargo/config.toml`:\\n\\n```toml\\n[target.x86_64-unknown-linux-musl]\\nlinker = \\\"x86_64-unknown-linux-musl-gcc\\\"\\n```\\n\\n4. Fetch and build:\\n```bash\\ncargo brazil fetch --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\\ncargo build --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl\\n```\\n\\n**Full guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-cross-compile/\\n\\n### Parallel Build Fleet Builds (Flexible Build)\\n\\nSpeed up pipelines by running test/lint phases in parallel with build:\\n\\n```bash\\n# Automated setup\\ntoolbox registry add 's3://buildertoolbox-registry-bt-rust-registry-us-west-2/tools.json'\\ntoolbox install cargo-brazil-migration-tool\\ncargo-brazil-migration-tool flexible-build \\\\\\n    --src-pkg MyPkg \\\\\\n    --vs MyVersionSet \\\\\\n    --delegate test \\\\\\n    --delegate lint\\n```\\n\\nThis creates separate packages for test/lint that run in parallel.\\n\\n**Full guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-build-speedup/\\n\\n### Environment Variables\\n\\n- `CARGO_BRAZIL_PREFER_LOCAL=1` - Prefer local over Brazil artifacts (useful for debugging)\\n- See [Debug Logging](#debug-logging) for `CARGO_BRAZIL_LOG` and `RUST_LOG` usage\\n\\n### Error Message Reference\\n\\n**\\\"ThirdPartyCratesIoIndex is N days old\\\"** - Your version set's crates mirror is stale. Merge from live or wait for sync.\\n\\n**\\\"amzn-* not found\\\"** - Missing `amzn-` prefix in Cargo.toml or missing Config dependency.\\n\\n**\\\"Could not find runtime environment\\\"** - Binary using ld-wrapper but no Apollo envroot. Use `.orig` binary or `brazil-runtime-exec`.\\n\\n**\\\"GLIBC_2.xx not found\\\"** - rust-analyzer binary too new for OS. Install via `axe init builder-tools` on AL2/CDD.\\n\\n**\\\"lock file interference\\\"** - Cargo.lock committed to git. Add to .gitignore and delete.\\n\\n## Resources\\n\\n- **CargoBrazil Docs:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil/\\n- **Dependency Guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-understanding-cargobrazil-dependencies/\\n- **Cross-Compile Guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-cross-compile/\\n- **Build Speedup Guide:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-build-speedup/\\n- **Extending CargoBrazil:** https://docs.hub.amazon.dev/languages/rust/cargobrazil-extend/\\n- **Rust Book:** https://doc.rust-lang.org/book/\\n- **Cargo Book:** https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"find c_src -maxdepth 2 -type f -printf '%p %s bytes\\\\\\\\n' | sort\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"find c_src -maxdepth 2 -type f -printf '%p %s bytes\\\\\\\\n' | sort\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"c_src/CMakeLists.txt 1200 bytes\\nc_src/src/main.c 1555 bytes\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"pwd; rg --files -g '\\\"'!target'\\\"' -g '\\\"'!build'\\\"'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"t