{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_60","type":"agent_message","text":"The remaining stack difference comes from linker policy, not function instructions: the C `.so` is lazily bound, while rustc marks the cdylib `BIND_NOW`. C’s first PLT resolutions leave data in the exact below-frame stack area that `bad` later reads. I’m aligning the Rust linker to lazy binding for this target so both libraries execute the same resolver path before the uninitialized access."}}
